  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 227 AM CDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nThis forecast package looks to remain mainly dry with a warming \ntrend through the period. Early this morning a surface ridge of high \npressure is building into the area. Clear skies and light winds are \nproviding a good radiational cooling set up allowing lows to start \nthis morning in the low to mid 30s. A freeze warning is in effect \nuntil 9AM for northeastern Kansas and northwest Missouri and a frost \nadvisory is in effect for much of he rest of the forecast area. After \nthe chilly start to the day, temperatures will remain below normal \ntoday as the area remains under cool northwest flow aloft and the \naforementioned surface ridge of high pressure remains in control. \nHowever, sunny skies will aid in temperatures rising into the upper \n50s to lower 60s. Tonight, the surface ridge axis will slide south \nand east of the area allowing weak warm advection to develop in its \nwake. This will hold low temperatures in the mid 30s to lower 40s \nkeeping patchy frost relegated to just sheltered area. Sunday, the \nupper level flow across the central CONUS will become quasi-zonal \nand weak WAA will continue at the surface. This will allow \ntemperatures to return to near seasonal normals with highs in the \nmid 60s. \n\nMonday, a upper level trough will look to dig into the west coast. \nThis will allow upstream, low-amplitude, upper level ridging to \nbuild over the central CONUS. This couple with continued modest WAA \nwill allow high to move back about normal in the upper 60s to lower \n70s. Tuesday, the upper level ridging across the central CONUS begin \nto become more amplified. These height rises will aid in high \nwarming into the low to mid 70s. Tuesday night, a shortwave rounding \nthe upper level ridge acts to briefly flatten the ridge and brings \nthe only really chance for isolated showers (less than 20%) to the \narea mainly across northern Missouri. Wednesday, the upper level \nridge continues to become more amplified pushing highs into the mid \n70s to the lower 80s. This upper level ridge will remain in control \nof the area through the remainder of the work week as WAA increases \nacross the area allowing high son Thursday and Friday to rise into \nthe upper 70s to mid 80s.",
